{"qid":"Q1196201","names":["Tuyuhun"],"title":"Tuyuhun","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Tuyuhun were a Xianbei-related semi-nomadic people who established a durable kingdom in the Qinghai-Tibetan plateau and surrounding steppes, attested from the late third century CE. At their height in the fifth and sixth centuries they controlled the critical Silk Road routes through the Hexi Corridor's southern flank, extracting tribute and trading horses with both Northern Wei and later Sui and Tang China. The Tang dynasty launched a major campaign in 634-635 under Emperor Taizong, decisively defeating the Tuyuhun ruler Murong Fuyun and installing a client king; the kingdom subsequently survived in reduced form until the Tibetan Empire absorbed it definitively around 663-665.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The Tuyuhun territorial extent is poorly attested by direct administrative records; it is reconstructed primarily from Chinese dynastic histories (Suishu, Jiu Tangshu) describing campaigns and tribute missions, supplemented by some archaeological evidence, making frontier boundaries substantially conjectural.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Tuyuhun","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Tuyuhun"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Tuyuhun","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tuyuhun"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Tuyuhun","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Murong Tuyuhun","nativeName":"慕容吐谷渾","roman":"Mùróng Tǔyùhún","start":284,"end":317,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Murong Tuyan","nativeName":"慕容吐延","roman":"Mùróng Tǔyán","start":317,"end":329,"image":nu